<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seleucid Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Seleucids, Seleucid</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Seleucid Empire was a Hellenistic state founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I Nicator</a>, one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>'s generals, after the break-up of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a>. At its height it stretched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucia">Seleucia</a>. It fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Empire</a>, ceded eastern lands to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>, lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a> and was finally annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 63 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire.jpg" alt="Seleucid Empire" width="320" height="157" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hartmann Linge · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:201209071746a_Berlin_Pergamonmuseum,_Tetradrachme_Seleukos%27_I,_Silber,_Pergamon,_281-280_v.u.Z.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
